  "transcript": "# Children and Young People's Mental Health\n\n## Purpose and expertise\n> The reviewers explained their backgrounds and why they were asked to run the independent review.\n\nChair: Professor Fonagy and Professor Baird, welcome. To start: why you were chosen for the review and your relevant experience?\nProfessor Fonagy: I'm a clinical psychologist with four decades in child mental health and have advised NHS England since 2011. That experience made me a natural choice to chair the review.\nProfessor Baird: I'm a paediatrician specialising in neurodisability, previously a consultant at Guy's and St Thomas'. I co‑chaired the review and have worked on guidelines and research relevant to ADHD, autism and related conditions.\n\n\n## A needs‑first model: top recommendations\n> They want a system that responds to a child's needs immediately, integrates assessments, focuses on prevention, and strengthens specialist care.\n\nChair: Briefly, what are your top recommendations and which are easiest to implement?\nProfessor Fonagy: We propose a shift from a diagnosis‑first system to a needs‑first system: identify and support needs as soon as a child presents, then prioritise diagnostic work if required.\nProfessor Fonagy: We also want stronger prevention, public‑health approaches to wellbeing, and integrated assessments because neurodevelopmental and mental health problems often co‑occur.\nProfessor Baird: Integration is crucial. Right now, separate pathways force multiple assessments and long waits. Better integration will reduce duplication and speed support.\nChair: You stress that specialist services must be protected, not downgraded.\nProfessor Fonagy: Exactly. Specialist assessment and treatment remain essential; the system just needs to start by addressing need immediately.\n\n\n## Why need is rising: social drivers\n> Reviewers reject the 'snowflake' idea and point to increased stress, less social support, social media and covid impacts as real drivers.\n\nPeter Swallow: Some say today's young people are just less resilient. You reject that—why?\nProfessor Fonagy: Calling them 'snowflakes' is insulting. Evidence shows more stress—financial insecurity, relentless social comparisons on social media, educational pressure—and less local youth provision since 2011.\nCaroline Voaden: How much do factors like social media and sleep each contribute? Where should government focus?\nProfessor Fonagy: They interact, so it's hard to quantify. But social media often displaces protective activities—sleep, exercise, face‑to‑face friendships. Policy should restore protective environments and encourage good habits.\nCaroline Voaden: Would delaying smartphone ownership help?\nProfessor Fonagy: Social norms matter more than bans. Practical parental guidance and creating alternatives—outdoor education, clubs—will be far more effective.\n\n\n## Diagnosis, misdiagnosis and the data gap\n> They found no evidence of large‑scale overdiagnosis but warned of misdiagnosis, data gaps since 2017, and the risks of long waits.\n\nGregory Stafford: If environment changes, could someone predisposed to ADHD not become disabled by it? Is it a continuum?\nProfessor Fonagy: Yes—it’s a continuum. ADHD is about attention regulation interacting with environment. Stressful settings make traits more disabling; early support can change outcomes.\nJoe Robertson: Your report gives a 1.2% autism population prevalence but >4% diagnosed. Isn't that overdiagnosis?\nProfessor Baird: We found no evidence of widespread overdiagnosis. Two points: we lack a recent population prevalence study in England (last comprehensive data is from 2017), and diagnostic understanding and thresholds have changed. So administrative diagnosis rates may not reflect 'overdiagnosis' of people with no problems.\nProfessor Fonagy: Overdiagnosis implies there is nothing wrong with people given labels. We see misdiagnosis and changing prevalence signals, but not proof that many people are wrongly labelled and have no need.\nChair: What must government do now to fill the evidence gap?\nProfessor Baird: Commission regular, up‑to‑date population prevalence studies and fund routine data collection so we can track change and evaluate reforms.\n\n\n## Services, schools and parents\n> They recommended integrated local neighbourhood hubs, better‑resourced school mental health teams, shared language between health and education, and regulating private assessments.\n\nJosh Fenton-Glynn: Parents seek private assessments because waits are so long. Does your model make that irrelevant?\nProfessor Fonagy: We don't recommend abolishing Right to Choose but prioritising NHS needs‑based support would reduce the incentive for expensive private assessments that don't always lead to follow‑up support.\nJosh Fenton-Glynn: Should private providers be regulated?\nProfessor Fonagy: Yes. Assessments that NHS pays for should be quality‑regulated—NICE standards and CQC oversight—so assessment equals useful next steps, not just a label.\nDarren Paffey: If we move to support‑first, what does that support look like?\nProfessor Baird: It must be evidence‑based, often psychosocial approaches, delivered earlier in school and community settings, and backed by NICE guidance. It can't be piecemeal or token online courses.\nJodie Gosling: Could needs‑first weaken access to statutory entitlements?\nProfessor Baird: Safeguards are necessary: integrated local services, clear prioritisation for complexity and risk, and parental confidence-building so diagnosis doesn't become the only route to support.\nSureena Brackenridge: What role should schools play in early identification?\nProfessor Fonagy: Schools are vital: young people rate family, friends and school as top supports. Mental health support teams should be empowered to cover the 'missing middle'—children who need more than basic support but less than specialist CAMHS.\nProfessor Baird: We back neighbourhood hubs co‑locating health, education and family support, and local commissioning that brings health into planning and action at school level.\nJen Craft: Would placing a legal duty on ICBs to deliver aspects of EHCP help?\nProfessor Fonagy: Yes. Health needs to be at the table and accountable—commissioning for outcomes like participation, not merely processes or diagnoses.\n\n\n## Workforce, digital tools and the single priority\n> They urged better training for school‑team clinicians, cautious use of digital/AI (clinician remains central), and their single top ask: join up services around children's needs.\n\nHelen Hayes: Mental health support teams need higher expertise. What workforce changes do you recommend?\nProfessor Fonagy: Train teams in proven therapies—CBT, trauma and eating‑disorder interventions—and boost capacity to treat the 'missing middle' rather than only mild cases.\nProfessor Baird: Co‑location and integrated commissioning would let teams access specialist advice quickly and stop families being pushed between services.\nChair: You mention digital assessments and AI; can you point to practical examples?\nProfessor Fonagy: Digital tools can give parents better information and help triage need. But: AI should describe and clinicians decide. There are pilot examples in parts of south London and Scotland, but national scale‑up needs careful testing and standards.\nProfessor Baird: Digital must be integrated with local services so advice leads to accessible help—not just signposting to commercial sites.\nChair: If you had to pick one recommendation for these Committees to press, what would it be?\nProfessor Fonagy: Bring services together around children's and families' needs—integrated neighbourhood hubs, shared language, joint commissioning and enough resources to respond early.\nProfessor Baird: I completely agree.\n",
